WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:10.538
In some cultures, sleep is considered a time when the soul is able to leave the body and traverse a spiritual universe, encountering long-dead friends and mystical creatures.

2
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.047
To these people, it is an important component of life.

3
00:00:14.048 --> 00:00:22.859
In other cultures, however, sleep is regarded as a waste of time, an unwelcome necessity that prevents people from working 24 hours a day.

4
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:29.621
Students cramming for exams often drink lots of coffee, which contains the stimulant caffeine, to keep awake.

5
00:00:29.622 --> 00:00:39.267
The American military is even researching a drug that will keep combat soldiers awake so they can do two forty-hour shifts of duty separated by eight hours of sleep.

6
00:00:39.268 --> 00:00:47.102
Work will not wait for the dreaming, they say. But no matter how hard people try to stay awake, eventually, sleep will prevail.

7
00:00:47.103 --> 00:00:52.925
After all, it is a fundamental part of the human experience─and it is inescapable.

8
00:00:52.926 --> 00:01:09.328
Scientists did not begin studying the phenomenon of sleep in earnest until the mid-twentieth century, when an American biologist named Franz Halberg noticed that animals seem to regulate their sleeping, eating, and waking behavior in a natural 24-hour pattern.

9
00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:12.404
Halberg called this pattern the "circadian rhythm."

10
00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:28.239
Through a series of studies on humans and other animals, Halberg and his team of experts determined that this cycle is both psychological and physiological; brainwave activity changes and the level of hormones in the body surges and declines, based on the time of day.

11
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:37.153
This pattern works like a daily schedule for the body, telling it when to perform certain tasks─one reason it is also known as the biological clock.

12
00:01:37.154 --> 00:01:47.877
In addition, Halberg found that exposure to sunlight is an external trigger that can prompt animals and humans to wake up and to look for food, while darkness tends to bring on sleep.

13
00:01:47.878 --> 00:01:54.281
While Halberg was running his studies, other scientists were beginning to research what happens in the body during sleep.

14
00:01:54.282 --> 00:02:03.872
They discovered that sleep can be clearly divided into two different types: REM (rapid eye movement) sleep and non-REM sleep.

15
00:02:03.873 --> 00:02:13.444
Throughout REM sleep, which often involves strange and random dreams, the eyes move briskly under closed lids, as if they are frantically reading a newspaper.

16
00:02:13.445 --> 00:02:21.691
Scientists believe that adults experience four or five periods of REM sleep during a normal night, about once every ninety minutes to two hours.

17
00:02:21.692 --> 00:02:30.262
Doctors have noticed that patients in REM mode show brainwave activity, irregular breathing, and heart rates similar to those of people who are awake.

18
00:02:30.263 --> 00:02:38.598
However, their motor neurons, cells that stimulate muscle movement, are dormant; therefore, the body remains completely still.

19
00:02:38.599 --> 00:02:45.984
In contrast to REM sleep, non-REM sleep is when our brains are inactive but our bodies are noticeably active.

20
00:02:45.985 --> 00:02:50.735
Non-REM sleep accounts for more than three-quarters of a human's total sleep time.

21
00:02:50.736 --> 00:03:00.993
It happens in four stages, with the first, known as "drowsy rest," occurring at the onset of sleep. People may twitch or their limbs may jerk involuntarily.

22
00:03:00.994 --> 00:03:06.305
Their brains stop producing "alpha waves," brainwaves that generally indicate active brain function.

23
00:03:06.306 --> 00:03:15.147
Then, as they lose awareness of their surroundings, they enter the second and longest stage, which can occupy more than half of all non-REM sleep time.

24
00:03:15.148 --> 00:03:29.217
Stages three and four, which represent the deepest periods of sleep, are when people might experience night terrors (episodes in which people experience intense fear, but that are usually not remembered in the morning), sleepwalking, and bed-wetting.

25
00:03:29.218 --> 00:03:34.380
It is extremely difficult to wake someone who is in stage four of non-REM sleep.

26
00:03:34.381 --> 00:03:41.873
The function of the different stages of sleep is not well understood, but several theories for the role of REM sleep have been proposed.

27
00:03:41.874 --> 00:03:52.367
One suggests that during this time, the brain consolidates memories, reinforcing information that it has learned during the day and incorporating it into long-term memory storage.

28
00:03:52.368 --> 00:03:55.106
This allows it to be retrieved at a later point.

29
00:03:55.107 --> 00:04:06.936
Another theory is that REM sleep is part of a recovery system that shuts down sensitive brain receptors that are constantly being damaged throughout the day and gives them time to rebuild and become healthy again.

30
00:04:06.937 --> 00:04:21.590
Alternatively, the ontogenetic hypothesis of REM sleep, which is based on the theory of how organisms develop from their earliest stages into adults, proposes that REM sleep plays an essential role in the development of the infant brain.

31
00:04:21.591 --> 00:04:29.669
According to this theory, REM sleep provides the neural stimulation that helps infants develop mature brains and nervous systems.

32
00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:33.998
Newborn babies experience longer periods of REM sleep than adults do.

33
00:04:33.999 --> 00:04:45.909
According to this theory, babies need more REM sleep because they receive an enormous amount of new information during waking hours compared with their parents, who already have a good understanding of how the world works.

34
00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:54.392
Some scientists also believe that if infants are regularly not allowed to sleep through the night, they will fail to develop a healthy brain later in life.

35
00:04:54.393 --> 00:05:03.808
However, what constitutes a good night of sleep is debatable. Most Western doctors think that adults should get at least eight hours each night.

36
00:05:03.809 --> 00:05:14.875
But many people have trouble staying asleep for a full night, and this has encouraged one researcher, Carol Worthman, to consider whether or not it is possible to sleep less and still remain functional.

37
00:05:14.876 --> 00:05:22.709
To explore this question, she visited several different communities in Africa, Europe, and Asia. What she discovered was startling.

38
00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:31.020
It seems that in many societies, instead of sleeping for an uninterrupted eight hours, people break up their sleeping time into two pieces.

39
00:05:31.021 --> 00:05:49.768
Members of one African tribe, for instance, go to bed at dusk for four hours (the first sleep), wake up for several minutes─during which time they check the safety of their children and feed the fire─and then fall back asleep for another four hours (the second sleep), eventually reviving around sunrise.

40
00:05:49.769 --> 00:05:59.169
In the Mediterranean, many people take short naps in the mid-afernoon when the temperature is too hot to work, and then sleep only four to six hours at night.

41
00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:04.087
Yet, both Africans and Mediterraneans feel well rested in the morning.

42
00:06:04.088 --> 00:06:10.156
There are reports of this kind of sleeping pattern in English-language literature written before industrialization.

43
00:06:10.157 --> 00:06:17.338
Worthman believes that as people began to work in factories with bright artificial lighting, their sleeping patterns shifted.

44
00:06:17.339 --> 00:06:26.895
Furthermore, she thinks that this transition from a two-part sleep to one long sleep is partially responsible for the increasing number of sleep disorders in Western society.

45
00:06:26.896 --> 00:06:33.986
Perhaps insomniacs are merely ignoring their circadian rhythms and trying to force themselves to sleep when they are not tired.

46
00:06:33.987 --> 00:06:42.557
People who are frustrated because they always wake up in the middle of the night may be experiencing the natural break between first and second sleeps.

47
00:06:42.558 --> 00:06:48.627
But however we sleep, it is a fact that all humans spend approximately a third of their lives slumbering.

48
00:06:48.628 --> 00:06:56.135
Some will nestle in comfortable beds, resting their heads on fluffy pillows. Others might stretch out on a blanket under the stars.

49
00:06:56.136 --> 00:07:01.705
Still others will lie down on thick cotton mattresses on the floor, covered with light quilts.

50
00:07:01.706 --> 00:07:07.591
Hopefully, all of them will get the rest their bodies crave before they have to get up and get back to waking life.

